		"comments": [
			{
				"type": "CSSComment",
				"value": " an `@`-word is a member of its own: the ordinary member gap stands on either\n\t\tside of it, whether or not the author wrote one, and its neighbours normalize ",
				"start": 17,
				"end": 179
			},
			{
				"type": "CSSComment",
				"value": " a group after an `@`-word is a second member, not an argument list — `@a` is no\n\t\tfunction name — so the ordinary gap stands there too, inside a function as well ",
				"start": 258,
				"end": 425
			},
			{
				"type": "CSSComment",
				"value": " an empty `@` is a member too, and keeps its authored glue to the group after it ",
				"start": 483,
				"end": 568
			},
			{
				"type": "CSSComment",
				"value": " an operator beside an `@`-word takes the gaps it takes beside any word ",
				"start": 599,
				"end": 675
			},
			{
				"type": "CSSComment",
				"value": " the word's own extent: it ends at whitespace, a quote, a comma, a paren, a\n\t\tbrace, a `/` or another `@`, so `*`, `+`, `-`, `:`, `#`, `.` and a digit are its\n\t\tcontent ",
				"start": 714,
				"end": 887
			},
			{
				"type": "CSSComment",
				"value": " a `-` straight before an `@`-word is an operator glued to it, at the head, after\n\t\tanother operator and inside a function alike ",
				"start": 975,
				"end": 1108
			}
		],
		"content": {
			"start": 7,
			"end": 1151,
			"styles": "\n\tdiv {\n\t\t/* an `@`-word is a member of its own: the ordinary member gap stands on either\n\t\tside of it, whether or not the author wrote one, and its neighbours normalize */\n\t\ta: a @b;\n\t\tb: @a @b;\n\t\tc: 1.5 @a;\n\t\td: #fff @a;\n\t\te: 'x' @a;\n\t\tf: @a 'x';\n\t\t/* a group after an `@`-word is a second member, not an argument list — `@a` is no\n\t\tfunction name — so the ordinary gap stands there too, inside a function as well */\n\t\tg: @a (2.5);\n\t\th: a @b (2.5);\n\t\ti: f(@a (2.5), 1.5);\n\t\t/* an empty `@` is a member too, and keeps its authored glue to the group after it */\n\t\tj: a @(2.5);\n\t\tk: @(2.5);\n\t\t/* an operator beside an `@`-word takes the gaps it takes beside any word */\n\t\tl: @a / 1.5;\n\t\tm: 1.5 / @a (2.5);\n\t\t/* the word's own extent: it ends at whitespace, a quote, a comma, a paren, a\n\t\tbrace, a `/` or another `@`, so `*`, `+`, `-`, `:`, `#`, `.` and a digit are its\n\t\tcontent */\n\t\tn: @a*1.50;\n\t\to: @a+1.50;\n\t\tp: @a-1.50;\n\t\tq: @a1.50;\n\t\tr: @a#FFF;\n\t\ts: f(@a:1.50);\n\t\t/* a `-` straight before an `@`-word is an operator glued to it, at the head, after\n\t\tanother operator and inside a function alike */\n\t\tt: -@a;\n\t\tu: 1.5 * -@a;\n\t\tv: f(-@a);\n\t}\n",
			"comment": null
		}
